--- a/docs/partners.md
+++ b/docs/partners.md
@@ -3,3 +3,14 @@
 Acme Logistics receives `order.created` webhooks and validates every payload against
 `schemas/webhook_order_v1.json` (strict: unknown fields are rejected and the delivery is
 dropped). Any new field needs a v2 schema and 30 days' notice to Acme.
+
+`order_created_payload(order)` continues to emit exactly v1 for CNY orders.
+It rejects EUR orders because v1 cannot communicate their currency.
+`order_created_payload(order, version=2)` emits the explicit currency using
+`schemas/webhook_order_v2.json`. The version is selected by the caller from the
+partner's agreed contract; it is not automatically selected from the order.
+
+Before enabling EUR intake for Acme, give the required 30 days' notice and confirm
+their v2 readiness, then configure the caller to request v2. No partner has been
+notified or activated by this code change. Routing/partner configuration is outside
+this repository; a serializer exception alone is not an intake feature gate.
diff --git a/docs/runbook.md b/docs/runbook.md
index ac9990b..d74d3d4 100644
--- a/docs/runbook.md
+++ b/docs/runbook.md
@@ -5,3 +5,75 @@
 - Adding a column with a NOT NULL default rewrites the whole table on Postgres 10 and
   locks it for the duration (last time: 47 minutes of failed checkouts). Add the column
   nullable, backfill in batches of 10k, then add the constraint.
+
+## Currency rollout
+
+Amounts remain integer minor units: fen for CNY, euro cents for EUR. Existing
+constructors default to CNY; new EUR orders must pass `currency="EUR"` explicitly.
+There is no FX conversion. `daily_revenue(orders)` requires CNY orders;
+`daily_revenue(eur_orders, currency="EUR")` requires EUR orders. Partition mixed
+input by currency before calling; a mixed total raises rather than summing money
+with different units. Keep the dashboard's currency label aligned with the argument.
+
+Prepare and rehearse the following stages on staging before production. This
+repository contains no database writer or migration runner; those integrations
+must persist/read currency explicitly before enabling EUR. During transition,
+map database NULL to CNY, consistent with all historical orders being CNY.
+
+1. Apply `migrations/0007_orders_currency.sql`. It adds a nullable column without
+   a default; the brief exclusive lock has a 2-second acquisition timeout. Retry
+   in a quiet period if it times out. This stage must precede writers using the column.
+2. Deploy all readers/writers with explicit currency persistence, including legacy
+   CNY writers. The cache namespace moves from v3 to v4: expect a cold cache and
+   monitor load. Finish rolling out old workers before EUR intake.
+3. Backfill historical NULL currencies as CNY, committing each batch of at most
+   10,000 rows. Use the query below, initially binding `:last_id` to NULL, then to
+   the returned `next_id`. Stop when `next_id` is NULL. The cursor assumes the
+   existing numeric primary key `id`; confirm this against the actual database.
+   Pause if checkout latency or replica lag rises. Run after all writers persist
+   currency, so no new NULL rows appear behind the cursor. Never overwrite an
+   explicit EUR value.
+
+   ```sql
+   WITH batch AS (
+       SELECT id FROM orders
+       WHERE (CAST(:last_id AS bigint) IS NULL OR id > CAST(:last_id AS bigint))
+       ORDER BY id LIMIT 10000
+   ), updated AS (
+       UPDATE orders AS o SET currency = 'CNY'
+       FROM batch WHERE o.id = batch.id AND o.currency IS NULL
+       RETURNING o.id
+   )
+   SELECT (SELECT max(id) FROM batch) AS next_id,
+          (SELECT count(*) FROM updated) AS updated_rows;
+   ```
+
+4. Once backfill is complete and all writers supply currency, verify
+   `SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE currency IS NULL OR currency NOT IN ('CNY', 'EUR');`
+   returns zero. Add a CHECK without scanning existing rows, then validate it in a
+   separate transaction (validation permits ordinary reads/writes but scans the table):
+
+   ```sql
+   SET lock_timeout = '2s';
+   ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT orders_currency_valid
+       CHECK (currency IS NOT NULL AND currency IN ('CNY', 'EUR')) NOT VALID;
+   RESET lock_timeout;
+   ```
+
+   ```sql
+   SET lock_timeout = '2s';
+   ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT orders_currency_valid;
+   RESET lock_timeout;
+   ```
+
+   The validated CHECK enforces non-null currencies. On Postgres 10, converting
+   this to column-level `SET NOT NULL` still scans under an exclusive lock; schedule
+   that separately in a maintenance window if required. Do not bundle it into the
+   additive migration. There is no database default, so stale writers fail visibly.
+5. Enable EUR only after database integration, finance labels and partner v2 rollout
+   are ready (see `partners.md`). No production migration or partner activation has
+   been performed here.
+
+To roll application code back after EUR exists, first stop EUR intake and retain
+currency-aware readers/reporting. Do not drop the column or let old code interpret
+EUR amounts as CNY. Rehearse recovery and cache load as part of staging rollout.
